我们当时在聊《时空穿越》马拉松。

00:17:10.369 --> 00:17:12.849
When else can you get away with that?
还有什么时候你能用这种梗？

00:17:12.849 --> 00:17:13.349
Yeah.
对啊。

00:17:13.349 --> 00:17:14.072
It's amazing.
太棒了。

00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:18.009
And it helps like the cell phones and- This is a historical document.
而且它还帮到了手机之类的——这是一份历史文献。

00:17:18.009 --> 00:17:20.089
But Craig, can we go back a little bit to this?
不过克雷格，我们能稍微回到这个话题吗？

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:38.930
Because I remember that during this episode, or maybe after this episode, I pulled you and Carter aside, and I said, I'm getting whiplash from going from an episode where Ted is the romantic hero, and it seems like his worldview is being validated and embraced by...
因为我记得在这集播出期间，或者可能是在这集之后，我把你和卡特拉到一边，我说：我感觉有点精神错乱，上一集泰德还是个浪漫英雄，他的世界观似乎得到了认可和支持……

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.369
And he's also a fucking badass in that episode.
而且他在那一集里简直是个该死的硬汉。

00:17:41.369 --> 00:17:44.210
He is like Han Solo and Lando Calrissian.
他就像韩·索罗和兰多·卡瑞西安。

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:47.450
We were making very a lot of Star Wars references when we talked about it.
我们在讨论时提到了很多《星球大战》的梗。

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:49.450
He's like very badass in that episode.
他在那一集里酷极了。

00:17:49.450 --> 00:18:01.470
I was less startled by it watching it now 20 years later, but I remember at the time feeling like Ted's romanticism in this episode, and even Victoria's romanticism is really held up for ridicule.
20年后的今天再看，我没有当年那么震惊，但我记得当时的感觉是，泰德在这集里的浪漫主义，甚至维多利亚的浪漫主义，真的都被拿来当笑柄嘲弄了。

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:07.450
Yeah, I know, and it's back to back with the best romantic comedy episode we arguably ever did, which is, I agree with this.
对，我知道，而且它紧接着我们做过的可以说是最棒的浪漫喜剧那一集后面播出的，对此我表示赞同。

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:09.049
I agree, it was whiplassy.
我同意，确实让人有点精神错乱。

00:18:09.049 --> 00:18:09.890
I agree.
我同意。

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:10.730
I agree.
我同意。

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:11.549
I agree.
我同意。

00:18:11.549 --> 00:18:12.390
I agree.
我同意。

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:15.970
I agree with some ways you were using this episode as a treacle cutter against drum roll, please.
我同意在某种程度上，你们用这一集来冲淡《请按卷首快进》里那种过于甜腻的感觉。

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:25.589
And that was the idea, and the idea was there, you think it's, these guys are so sort of smoopy and gooey on the couch, but really it's to get martial melody to a place of, why don't we have firsts?
这就是我们的想法，当时的构思是，你觉得这些人坐在沙发上腻腻歪歪、黏黏糊糊的，但实际上是为了让马歇尔和莉莉陷入一种状态：为什么我们没有这种“第一次”了？

00:18:25.589 --> 00:18:26.490
What's happening to us?
我们身上发生了什么？

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:37.509
Why are we ridiculing this sweetness when we should be holding on to the sweetness and then realizing they're sort of into a new phase and that's okay and you will still find firsts like peeing in front of each other and we thought that was funny.
为什么我们要嘲笑这种甜蜜，而我们明明应该去守护这种甜蜜呢？然后他们意识到自己正在进入一个新阶段，这其实没关系，而且你们还是会找到新的“第一次”，比如在对方面前撒尿，我们觉得这挺搞笑的。

00:18:37.509 --> 00:18:42.789
But it's so Ted and Victoria really there is the object, not the subject of the story.
但泰德和维多利亚在这里真的只是故事的客体，而不是主体。

00:18:42.789 --> 00:18:47.509
They were very much the stars of the preceding story and they were gonna be the stars of the next several.
他们在前一集里绝对是主角，而且他们也将成为接下来好几集的主角。

00:18:47.509 --> 00:18:55.230
And so we thought, oh, we'll take them off of that and we'll get into, I mean, Marshall and Lily, and then this is the first time we saw Barney and Robin together, and they were so great together.
所以我们想，哦，我们要把他们从那种状态中抽离出来，然后转到——我是说马歇尔和莉莉身上，接着这是我们第一次看到巴尼和罗宾在一起，他们俩在一起太棒了。

00:18:55.230 --> 00:19:01.990
It's first time Barney talks about the bro code, and Barney learns this really important piece of information that Robin is harboring these feelings for Ted.
这是巴尼第一次谈到“兄弟守则”，而且巴尼得知了一个非常重要的情报：罗宾对泰德怀有这种感情。

00:19:01.990 --> 00:19:03.589
I like that Barney sees that.
我喜欢巴尼看出来了这一点。

00:19:03.589 --> 00:19:05.509
I like that Robin admits that to Ted.
我喜欢罗宾向泰德承认这一点。

00:19:05.509 --> 00:19:09.549
I like that she swears in the secrecy, and he says, you're a bro, it's the bro code, I'll keep it in.
我喜欢她发誓要保守秘密，而他说：你是好兄弟，根据兄弟守则，我会保守秘密的。

00:19:09.549 --> 00:19:12.509
And that became a huge thing on the show, but it began right there.
这后来成了剧中一个极其重要的梗，但它就是从这里开始的。

00:19:12.509 --> 00:19:14.230
Quid pro bro was great.
“投桃报李之兄弟版”太棒了。

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:15.710
Quid pro bro is amazing.
“投桃报李之兄弟版”绝了。

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:18.910
And that sort of launched, you know, what we'd end up doing books of those.
这某种程度上开启了，你知道的，我们后来甚至为此出了书。

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:29.630
Um, but I really, I, I do feel bad because in the, in trying to do all those other things, I think Ted and Victoria became a caricature for just this one episode and they've been heroes the one before.
呃，不过我真的，我真的觉得有点抱歉，因为在试图兼顾所有这些其他事情的过程中，我觉得泰德和维多利亚在这一集里变成了一个刻板形象，而他们在前一集里可是英雄。

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:37.990
I'll let you off the hook because everything you're describing, I think it's actually right and good and I understand why you were doing it as writers and I could make a case and I think this is true.
我放你一马，因为你描述的一切，我觉得其实完全正确、非常好，我理解你们作为编剧当时为什么要这么做，而且我可以举证，我觉得这也是事实。

00:19:37.990 --> 00:19:47.029
Like, if we were eavesdropping on Ashley and Neil on the boardwalk eating junk food and making jokes, we'd be like, Oh God, this couple.
比如，如果我们偷听阿什莉和尼尔在木板路上吃垃圾食品并开玩笑，我们也会想：天哪，这对情侣。

00:19:47.029 --> 00:20:01.710
And when you're inside that, when I'm holding Jordana's hand across the table, my parents are looking at me like we're insane, but when you're inside the new flush of love or potential love, it all seems completely organic, completely right and true and good.
当你身处其中时，当我隔着桌子握着乔丹娜的手时，我父母看着我们的眼神就像我们疯了一样，但当你身处新恋情或潜在恋情的甜蜜冲动中时，这一切看起来都完全自然、完全正确、真实而美好。

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:02.710
Yeah.
对啊。

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:10.509
Like there's nothing about it to you, but from the outside, especially couples have been together a long time or more cynical people, it elicits a lot of eye rolls.
就好像对你来说这没什么，但从局外人的角度来看，尤其是对那些在一起很久的情侣或者比较愤世嫉俗的人来说，这会招来无数的白眼。

00:20:10.509 --> 00:20:11.589
So I get why you did it.
所以我理解你们为什么这么做。

00:20:11.589 --> 00:20:46.630
I was thinking about how this, how you felt this, because you told me this in a previous episode and we said we'd discuss it when we got here and here we are discussing it, it almost feels like we leave Future Ted's point of view in the storyline and go into Marshall and Lily's point of view and it's purely Marshall and Lily go like can you believe these rookies, these fucking embarrassing amateur hour new couple over here and then they sort of realized that that reflects badly on them in some way that they're not, you know, so it almost feels like we shift out of Future Ted for a minute to just be like them how they're seeing Ted in Victoria, rather than 10 Victoria, were actually quite that overboard.
我在想你是怎么看待这一点的，因为你在之前的一集里跟我说过这个，当时我们说等聊到这集时再讨论，现在我们正在讨论它；这感觉几乎就像我们在剧情中暂时离开了“未来泰德”的视角，切入了马歇尔和莉莉的视角，纯粹是马歇尔和莉莉在想：你敢相信这些菜鸟、这些该死的新手期丢脸的业余情侣吗？然后他们某种程度上意识到这在某种程度上也反映了他们自身的某些问题，你知道的，他们也有不足；所以感觉我们好像暂时跳出了未来泰德的视角，仅仅去展现他们是如何看待泰德和维多利亚的，而不是说泰德和维多利亚当时真的表现得那么过头。

00:20:46.630 --> 00:20:53.990
Did you feel any of that, Ashley, like going from the romanticism of drumroll into the kind of more comedy of Zip-Zip-Zip?
阿什莉，你有没有这种感觉，就是从《卷首快进》的浪漫主义过渡到《滋滋滋》这种偏喜剧的风格？

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:56.029
Well, that's, you know, what you just said is it.
嗯，这就是，你知道的，你刚才说的就是关键所在。

00:20:56.029 --> 00:20:59.390
Like, I think it's always important.
我觉得这永远都很重要。

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:06.829
And, you know, I say this, like across all mediums of television, it's really important to remember what genre you're in.
而且，你知道，我一直这么说，在电视的所有媒介中，记住你身处什么类型片是非常重要的。

00:21:06.829 --> 00:21:14.430
So you were kind of genre-bending a bit with the multi-cam platform in drum roll, please.
所以在《请按卷首快进》中，你们用多机位平台做了一点跨类型（genre-bending）的尝试。

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:17.789
And this was, hey, don't worry.
而这一集则是：嘿，别担心。

00:21:17.789 --> 00:21:25.069
We're just gonna take it up one little bit and exaggerate it just a little bit so that you know this is still all fun.
我们只是把基调稍微往上提了一点点，稍微夸张了一点点，好让你们知道这一切都还是很好玩的。

00:21:25.069 --> 00:21:28.150
And we're still gonna get laughs.
而且我们依然会逗笑观众。

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:41.109
And ultimately that's why I think the timeline that you guys have across all episodes really works well because it is taking certain things out of context, right?
归根结底，这就是为什么我觉得你们贯穿所有剧集的这种时间线处理方式效果极佳，因为它把某些事情抽离出了语境，对吧？

00:21:41.109 --> 00:21:48.549
So there's these, you know, anything that we take out of context, it's you can lean it in any direction.
所以有这些，你知道的，任何我们抽离语境的东西，你都可以把它往任何方向引导。

00:21:48.549 --> 00:22:01.309
So you took these little moments of, you know, I want to feel your soul and cut to, you know, that little excerpt and cut to them eye roll, you know, but in context, it feels really organic.
所以你们截取了这些小片段，你知道的，比如“我想感受你的灵魂”，然后切到，你知道的，那个小片段，接着切到他们翻白眼，你知道的，但在具体的语境中，它感觉非常自然。

00:22:01.309 --> 00:22:07.309
And that is one of the brilliant, you know, conceits of the show in terms of the editing and the structure.
这就是这部剧在剪辑和结构方面最精彩的巧妙构思之一。

00:22:07.309 --> 00:22:12.029
But, you know, I also, I am a soldier to a genre.
但是，你知道，我对某种类型片也是绝对忠诚的。

00:22:12.029 --> 00:22:15.190
So for me, I was like, oh, good, we're back.
所以对我来说，我会觉得：哦，太好了，我们回来了。

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:16.869
We're back in the comedy world.
我们回到了喜剧的世界。

00:22:16.869 --> 00:22:20.230
You know, and that's, that's why I loved that.
你知道，这就是为什么我很喜欢这一点。

00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:23.789
It was just a, just a one, one tick exaggerated.
它只是，仅仅把夸张度调高了一个刻度而已。

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:24.410
Yeah.
对啊。

00:22:24.410 --> 00:22:24.910
Yeah.
对啊。

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:26.750
Well, thank you guys for committing to it.
好吧，谢谢你们对这个设定的坚持。

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:27.750
No, of course.
不，当然。

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:29.750
And cheesy lines.
还有那些老套的台词。

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:48.709
And since this is the zip, zip, zip, you know, episode of this podcast, I also just want to say like, it might not be the most, you know, 10 Victoria forward episode, but this is the episode where they jumped in the sack, which is monumental in terms of the trajectory of the relationship.
既然这是那个“拉链、拉链、拉链”（Zip, Zip, Zip）的播客单集，我其实也想说，它可能不是那种泰德和维多利亚戏份最多的单集，但这一集里他们俩突破了最后防线，这对于他们关系的轨迹来说具有里程碑式的意义。

00:22:48.709 --> 00:22:53.630
So let's not downplay zip zip zip in terms of the story arc of 10 Victoria.
所以，就《老爸老妈浪漫史》中泰德和维多利亚的故事线而言，我们可别低估了“拉链、拉链、拉链”的重要性。

00:22:53.630 --> 00:23:16.869
I think Craig, I was going through a little bit of like, I was still trying to figure out who I think maybe it was a little bit of ego, like when you're so kind of plate dropped in the center of the episode and driving the episode, you're the emotional and narrative energy of an episode, and then you become, like, Ted and Victoria are almost like the C-story.
克雷格，我觉得我当时有点……我还在努力摸索自己——可能也有点自我作祟吧，就像你作为核心被放在单集的正中间推动剧情，你是一集当中情感与叙事的能量所在，然后你突然变得……泰德和维多利亚几乎成了C线剧情。

00:23:16.869 --> 00:23:17.869
They are.
确实是。

00:23:17.869 --> 00:23:18.869
They kind of are accepted.
差不多就是这样。

00:23:18.869 --> 00:23:23.789
They also make this one a huge leave then, which gives the weight to the story, right?
但它们也为这个故事带来了巨大的喘息空间，赋予了故事厚重感，对吧？

00:23:23.789 --> 00:23:24.789
Yeah.
对。

00:23:24.789 --> 00:23:33.589
maybe I was having a response of like, like a little bit of a like a serotonin dip of coming down off of drum roll, you know?
也许我当时有那种反应，就像从《击鼓传花》的高潮中退烧后，出现了一点小小的血清素（快乐激素）戒断反应，你懂吗？

00:23:33.589 --> 00:23:43.069
And feeling like, oh, I'm not, it's not always, my story and my character is not always going to be centered and spot lit and lionized, you know?
就会觉得，哦，我不是……我的故事和我的角色并不是永远都处于中心、备受瞩目并被当成英雄来崇拜的，你知道吗？

00:23:43.069 --> 00:23:53.430
But I did feel some protection of him and maybe actually you've had this in other roles you've played but like you're, you're the guardian or gatekeeper of your character.
但我确实对他产生了一种保护欲，也许你在扮演其他角色时也有过这种感觉，就是你是你角色的守护者或把关人。

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:56.789
And sometimes, you know, I don't believe in like, Mark Kirkler would never say this.
有时候，你知道，我不相信……马特·库克兰（Matt Kuhn）绝对不会这么说。

00:23:56.789 --> 00:24:03.509
But sometimes when you have a good working symbiotic relationship with the writers, you are co-creating the thing, right?
但有时，当你与编剧们建立起良好的、共生的工作关系时，你其实是在共同创作这个作品，对吧？

00:24:03.509 --> 00:24:04.349
Oh, definitely.
哦，绝对是。

00:24:04.349 --> 00:24:09.630
So I really wanted to say like, I want this guy's vulnerability to be heroic.
所以我真的很想说：我希望这家伙的脆弱能显得英勇。

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:13.430
I want his romanticism to be applauded.
我希望他的浪漫主义能受到喝彩。

00:24:13.430 --> 00:24:26.829
And I was worried that, and it's the same thing I felt when Ted's intellect started being ridiculed, which is something that I like value, you know, people's who read books and engage with ideas.
我当时担心的是——这和我当初感觉到的一样，当泰德的才智开始被嘲弄时——这也是我很珍视的东西，你知道的，那些喜欢读书、关注各种思想的人。

00:24:26.829 --> 00:24:31.750
And there was a kind of anti-intellectual streak that went through how I met your mother as it went on.
随着《老爸老妈浪漫史》的播出，剧中曾出现过一种反智主义的倾向。

00:24:31.750 --> 00:24:33.349
We can get into that later.
我们稍后可以细聊这个。

00:24:33.349 --> 00:24:41.150
But it was always hard for me because I felt like these things that I actually wanted to be valued were sometimes thrown under the bus.
但这对我来说总是很难接受，因为我觉得这些我真正希望被珍视的特质有时却被无情地抛弃了。

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:47.029
Yeah, I mean, and then they're heroic traits one episode and then not the next, which is a little whiplashy.
对，我的意思是，他们这一集还是英雄特质，下一集就不是了，这让人感觉有点像头部受了鞭笞般错愕。

00:24:47.029 --> 00:24:49.750
And yeah, we're still finding it, right?
而且，是的，我们当时仍在摸索，对吧？

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:58.630
is the middle of season one, we're still finding like the idea of laying out a whole season of stories and the tone shifts and where the characters are each episode and getting them across episodes.
这是第一季的中段，我们还在摸索如何铺陈一整季的故事、拿捏色调的转变、安排每个角色在每集中的走向，以及如何将他们贯穿于各个单集之间。

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:00.558
And I think we were still finding it.
我觉得我们当时还在探索中。

00:25:00.558 --> 00:25:24.269
And if I, yeah, I think if I could do it over again, I would probably, well, first of all, I'd have more Ashley, I've had more Victoria episodes and maybe a couple in between drumroll and this one, where there was like some kind of different speed that sort of like ramps down rather than being way up high as the heroes and then way down in a a different kind of sillier place, and that's probably even more pronounced the way people now watch it, right?
如果我……对，我觉得如果能重来一次，我大概会……首先，我会安排更多阿什利……更多维多利亚的戏份，也许在《击鼓传花》和这一集之间再加几集，让节奏有一种不同的过渡，慢慢缓下来，而不是一下子作为英雄冲到最高点，然后又落到一个不同且更显愚蠢的地方。现在人们看这部剧的方式，可能让这种感觉更加明显了，对吧？

00:25:24.269 --> 00:25:34.509
You at least had a week between the two Monday nights when you're watching it on TV back in 2006, and now it's like you just watch them and it probably feels even more pronounced in a way.
在2006年电视上追剧时，两个周一之间你至少还能隔一周，而现在大家都是连着看，这种落差感在某种程度上可能就更强烈了。

00:25:34.509 --> 00:25:43.269
But I think we're still finding some of that larger, the characters over time through a whole 22 or 24 episode arc, and we're still finding it.
但我认为我们仍在摸索如何通过整整22集或24集的宏大故事线来塑造那些宏观的角色，我们当时还在探索。

00:25:43.269 --> 00:25:48.349
